Vulnerability Title

Exposure of Usernames in Client-Side JavaScript Source Code

Risk Level

Low

CVSS v3.1 Score (Intranet Application)

3.7 – Low

Vector String:
CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:N/A:N

Vulnerability Category

Information Disclosure

OWASP Category

OWASP Top 10 2021 – A02: Cryptographic Failures (Sensitive Data Exposure)
https://owasp.org/Top10/A02_2021-Cryptographic_Failures/

Vulnerability Description

During the security assessment, it was observed that the application’s client-side JavaScript file (app.js) contains references to internal usernames. These usernames are exposed within the source code that is publicly accessible through the browser.

Any user can view the JavaScript file by inspecting the page source or directly accessing the app.js file from the application. The exposed usernames may belong to internal employees or application users.

Although this does not immediately allow direct system compromise, exposing internal usernames increases the risk of user enumeration and targeted attacks such as phishing or brute-force attempts against user accounts.

Impact

Disclosure of internal usernames to unauthorized users.

Attackers can use the exposed usernames for credential brute-force attacks.

Increases the risk of targeted phishing or social engineering attacks.

May assist attackers in identifying valid accounts within the system.

Proof of Concept (PoC) Steps

Step 1: Open the application in a web browser.

Step 2: Right-click on the page and select Inspect or press F12 to open developer tools.

Step 3: Navigate to the Sources or Network tab.

Step 4: Locate and open the JavaScript file named app.js.

Step 5: Review the contents of the file and observe that internal usernames are present within the source code.

Step 6: Alternatively, access the file directly via the browser using the URL:
https://<application-domain>/path/app.js

Step 7: Confirm that the usernames are visible in the JavaScript code.

Recommendations

Avoid storing sensitive information such as usernames in client-side JavaScript files.

Move sensitive configuration data to secure server-side storage.

Implement code minimization and obfuscation techniques to reduce exposure of internal details.

Review all client-side scripts to ensure no sensitive information is exposed.

Follow secure coding practices to prevent information disclosure.

